  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "1.0.0-preview.9: Concurrency + lifecycle hardening on InMemoryReplayCache and HttpPqJwtKeyRing. SECURITY: InMemoryReplayCache.Prune removed entries by key alone, so a concurrent TryRegister that replaced a just-expired entry with a fresh expiresAt could see its LIVE entry deleted — and the next presentation of the same jti could slip past replay defense. Now uses atomic compare-and-remove. RELIABILITY: HttpPqJwtKeyRing disposed MLDsa handles inline during cache replacement, racing concurrent Resolve callers mid-VerifyData and crashing them with ObjectDisposedException; now uses a 30s deferred-disposal quarantine queue. BREAKING (preview-tier): HttpPqJwtKeyRing is now an IHostedService — Resolve is a pure in-memory lookup, the cache is refreshed by a background loop. Consumers must register the type as both a singleton AND a hosted service: `services.AddSingleton(sp => new HttpPqJwtKeyRing(...)); services.AddHostedService(sp => sp.GetRequiredService<HttpPqJwtKeyRing>());`. The previous sync-over-async Resolve risked thread-pool starvation under load. Also: skips MLDsa reimport when published base64 is unchanged (steady-state native-handle churn drops from one-per-poll to zero). Tokens produced by this library's builder are unaffected; the public API surface for token issue/validate is unchanged. Production-oriented preview: NOT independently audited, NOT a drop-in OAuth/OIDC/JWT replacement. Full notes: https://github.com/systemslibrarian/postquantum-jwt/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md — known limitations: https://github.com/systemslibrarian/postquantum-jwt/blob/main/KNOWN-GAPS.md.",
